00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:30.450
ID so we can look it up
and get history later.

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:35.392
Errors, whenever
there's an error and

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:36.990
just throughout the work.

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:43.398
Okay, and the tasks
is the other part of this

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:48.090
so, these are the
specs, basically.

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:50.790
They're to-do tasks.

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:54.090
Started out as a little
to-do list I had the

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:57.986
AI maintain for me,
and then it became-- you know,

00:13:57.986 --> 00:14:00.988
I add more and more detail
until it became,

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:03.920
you know, documents
of their own.

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:08.610
And this workflow, you know,
kind of just evolved

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:14.100
organically as I developed
using these, using the system.

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:21.660
So, you know, in the-- the--
the code base I'm sharing

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:26.293
with you, it's-- they're all
kept inside this to-do folder,

00:14:26.723 --> 00:14:32.671
that keeps a master list of
all tasks in the to-do index.

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:36.810
There's a specific
numbering scheme for tasks.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:41.610
It even keeps track of priority
and the status of the tasks.

00:14:42.037 --> 00:14:44.010
I'll show that in
the code later.

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:49.179
And, you know,
it's an iterative

00:14:49.179 --> 00:14:53.352
process, creating this,
these tasks with the AI.

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:58.740
So like, start out--You know,
the task, it what you want it

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:03.840
to do, everything it needs to
know to create this task.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:09.360
You start out just
telling it what you want.

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:16.482
It has instructions built in
to the prompt to the Claude

00:15:16.482 --> 00:15:18.756
code like, claude.md .md

00:15:20.431 --> 00:15:23.838
Like cloud,
the md file that

00:15:25.278 --> 00:15:26.691
allows it to understand

00:15:26.691 --> 00:15:29.124
how to create tasks,
and you know, like,

00:15:29.124 --> 00:15:32.324
what to do with them,
and then you review.

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:33.916
So once it creates
the initial version,

00:15:33.916 --> 00:15:36.540
you go through a review,
and it's just iterative.

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:38.160
Like, look at the document.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:39.894
If there's something
there that you don't want,

00:15:40.171 --> 00:15:40.950
tell it to remove it.

00:15:41.393 --> 00:15:44.310
If there's something there
it needs, you add it until,

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:48.872
you know, you're happy with it,
and then after that is

00:15:48.912 --> 00:15:53.850
the build process where
it's also iterative.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:58.200
You know, with these games,
you know, I'll show you a few,

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:01.890
it creates a lot of them
in one shot pretty well.

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:04.993
Some of them,
like Mario and Pac-Man

00:16:04.993 --> 00:16:07.452
will have a little, some bugs,
but you know,

00:16:07.452 --> 00:16:09.354
that's the nature of these,
you know,

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:12.559
non-deterministic systems.

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:14.700
It's gonna create
a different game every time.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.850
So I've run this,
you know, in testing,

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:20.820
like, half a dozen times,
and each time the game

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:21.570
is a little different.

00:16:24.229 --> 00:16:28.187
So anyways, you know,

00:16:28.682 --> 00:16:31.246
so you work with AI until

00:16:31.246 --> 00:16:35.525
the implementation is what
you want, and then, you know,

00:16:35.525 --> 00:16:39.755
deploy, test, document,
and eventually archive

00:16:39.786 --> 00:16:44.529
the task, which has instructions
on how to do that explicitly.

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:47.856
Guides.

00:16:48.362 --> 00:16:52.230
So, these are like
documents teaching it

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:53.902
how to do certain things.

00:16:54.780 --> 00:17:02.950
So, this could be how to debug
webpage, how to patch something.

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:06.469
Like, for instance,
one of the websites I work on,

00:17:06.768 --> 00:17:09.786
patching is a really
tedious process.

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:16.079
I've created a guide, and the
AI reads the guide, and then

00:17:16.079 --> 00:17:17.520
it does the patching for me.

00:17:18.089 --> 00:17:21.059
You know, there's a few,
it might not get

00:17:21.511 --> 00:17:25.654
all the patches done,
but it does 90% by itself,

00:17:28.884 --> 00:17:31.136
and I'll be demonstrating that.

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:33.376
As well,
later on.

00:17:36.309 --> 00:17:39.541
One of the issues I
found with guides is security.

00:17:40.033 --> 00:17:45.090
So, I create a custom
script that checks to

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:50.370
see if there's, you know,
secrets in the guide, you know,

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:51.960
passwords or other things.

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:55.255
'Cause it really likes to put
everything into this guide.

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:57.720
So you have to be
a little diligent.

00:17:57.720 --> 00:17:59.460
You have to check,
make sure it's not

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:01.710
doing anything,
checking in stuff

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:02.739
that shouldn't be there.

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:07.530
And, you know, having
an automated script go

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:11.700
through helps it do that
before coming to you.

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:15.930
So you can say, you know,
run the verification script.

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:19.740
It will go and then
put placeholders

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:23.330
itself before you even,
you know, need to review it.

00:18:25.871 --> 00:18:29.385
And then testing is another
part of this system.

00:18:32.691 --> 00:18:36.323
The testing is kind
of a guardrail.

00:18:37.598 --> 00:18:40.424
So, it's a guardrail,

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:43.198
it also helps it during
the development process.

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:51.245
So, having the AI create these
tests helps it,

00:18:51.769 --> 00:18:53.984
you know, first of all helps
you be more confident,

00:18:53.984 --> 00:18:56.056
which goes back to
that trust part,

00:18:56.484 --> 00:18:59.982
getting you above the 46% trust.

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:04.374
But it also, I found,
has side benefits in terms

00:19:04.374 --> 00:19:07.953
of keeping the AI focused
while it's doing the work.

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:13.593
So you can tell it, you know,
make a test for, you know,

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:17.430
making sure the items are
selectable, making sure,

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:18.864
you know, this or that work.

00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:20.790
And it will put
that in the code,

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:24.240
and then it will
automatically run the

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:27.012
tests and find the issues
before even coming to you.

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:31.577
So it helps make the iteration a
lot faster.

00:19:31.711 --> 00:19:33.601
The AI iterates with itself

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:38.070
for a while, and then you can
come back and do the real

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:42.180
testing and see, you know,
if it's up to snuff.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:44.410
And you can even ask
it to make more tests,

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:48.981
which is also helpful
to prevent regressions.

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:54.812
'Cause often the AI will fix one
thing and then break another.

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:58.560
Or it'll fix one thing,
and then you'll add

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:00.390
another feature,
but it will put

00:20:00.390 --> 00:20:02.379
back the same
broken code.

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:06.219
And it doesn't make any
sense, I find, you know,

00:20:06.219 --> 00:20:08.456
if you'd make it, if you
have it write a test.

00:20:09.048 --> 00:20:10.878
You don't get
that regression.

