00:39:08.279 --> 00:39:12.177
[AUDIENCE MEMBER] Somebody was
asking about the specs, right?

00:39:12.318 --> 00:39:15.499
How much time you spend on it
and how do you approach--

00:39:16.558 --> 00:39:17.078
[TRUCKER HATCH] Yeah.

00:39:17.078 --> 00:39:23.550
So basically,
a lot of my work

00:39:23.550 --> 00:39:26.610
is now creating
the specs, you know,

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:30.480
I have to read and make
sure that they have all

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:31.830
the things that are required.

00:39:32.220 --> 00:39:35.552
So you know,
it's a lot more

00:39:35.552 --> 00:39:39.360
reading and reviewing,
but it's a lot-

00:39:40.023 --> 00:39:40.560
[JON GREEN] Faster.

00:39:40.560 --> 00:39:43.880
You can find like,
you write the spec and you

00:39:43.880 --> 00:39:48.141
can find the bugs, you know,
before you write the code, you

00:39:48.141 --> 00:39:51.931
can look at the architecture,
can make sure it doesn't create

00:39:51.931 --> 00:39:54.287
something, you know, wonky.

00:39:56.345 --> 00:40:00.840
You think at a higher level,
you know, it's not so much nuts

00:40:00.840 --> 00:40:02.370
and bolts of the code anymore.

00:40:02.430 --> 00:40:06.360
It's more like,
what do I want to create?

00:40:06.390 --> 00:40:08.424
How's a good design for that?

00:40:09.090 --> 00:40:14.586
So it's a lot more of the work,
but it pays off in the end

00:40:16.098 --> 00:40:18.150
you know, in terms of the
speed of the development.

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:22.606
With these AI agents,
you can pivot a lot quicker.

00:40:22.606 --> 00:40:25.020
You know, if you write,
like my example,

00:40:25.020 --> 00:40:26.317
I wrote the code in

00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:31.710
a language that didn't
have a library that was

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:36.200
as powerful as I needed for
the features that were coming.

00:40:37.140 --> 00:40:39.388
I just changed the language.

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:41.558
I wrote a spec,
I told it, "You know,

00:40:41.580 --> 00:40:44.640
we need to change the
language to this one

00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:46.070
using this library."

00:40:46.350 --> 00:40:50.068
And it went through,
looked at the code in

00:40:50.070 --> 00:40:53.760
the other language,
translated it and put

00:40:53.760 --> 00:40:56.103
all the requirements
that I asked it to put

00:40:56.460 --> 00:41:00.557
in the spec into the code.

00:41:01.080 --> 00:41:02.600
So, yeah.

00:41:14.279 --> 00:41:14.799
Yeah.

00:41:14.799 --> 00:41:18.304
So people are, you know,
I-- oh, sorry.

00:41:19.494 --> 00:41:21.198
So the question was

00:41:22.805 --> 00:41:25.620
"Is my interaction with
the code like a black box?"

00:41:26.280 --> 00:41:30.071
And I still, you know,
read the code.

00:41:30.090 --> 00:41:32.177
I still make sure it's good.

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:36.780
I still write tests
or check the tests,

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:42.570
but it's less about

00:41:45.030 --> 00:41:46.890
writing code,
for sure, you know.

00:41:47.310 --> 00:41:51.300
It's more like, "Okay,
this code looks good," you know.

00:41:52.140 --> 00:41:55.110
"This code has all the
things I asked for it,

00:41:55.860 --> 00:41:57.540
